    {
      "repository": "anomalyco/hex",
      "url": "https://github.com/anomalyco/hex",
      "trending_rank": 10,
      "description": "A ==push-to-talk dictation app for macOS, Windows and Linux==: hold a shortcut, speak, let go, and the text is transcribed on your device and pasted into the app you are in. You can pick among several local speech models, add your own model files, keep a vocabulary of names per mode, and use opt-in meeting transcription. A command-line tool lets coding agents read retained transcripts and control the running app.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. We passed on it on 2 November because it overlapped Handy, the local dictation app we picked that day; since then v3.3.0 (2 November) added ==a side-by-side model picker, per-mode vocabularies== and a Russian model. Its difference from Handy is the agent side: transcripts and app control from the command line, and an Annotate mode that records your screen while you talk and sends it to an agent.",
      "caveat": "The README says nothing leaves your machine after the first speech-model download; that is the maintainers' claim, not something we tested. The macOS build needs Apple silicon and macOS 15, and ==Windows and Linux are labelled beta==. Its security notes say custom command handlers and npm dependencies ==run with your permissions and are not sandboxed==. Unfinished features sit in a Labs switch that is on by default, releases arrive several times a day, and the project does not take pull requests. We did not install it.",
      "category": "Productivity / speech-to-text",
      "language": "Rust",
      "license": "MIT",
      "stars_total": 4760,
